  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-0496ba9f Web logs overview\"** (`eff-0496ba9f-web-logs-overview-c9fabe76dbae`)\n- Total requests (count)\n- Bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Server errors (5xx) (ES|QL metric)\n\nThe time range is absolute, 2026-10-01 to 2026-12-05. The sample data runs from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, which is ahead of today's date (2026-10-11), so a \"last 7 days\" window would show nothing.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-0496ba9f 5xx spike\"** (`a2961bd6-7f9d-4e38-abb7-d54e29c36f9b`)\n- Type `.es-query`, runs every 1m, enabled, status `ok`.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5??` in a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Tagged `EFF-0496ba9f`. I added the tag myself; it wasn't in the request.\n- No action connector is attached, so the rule records matches but doesn't notify anyone.\n\n**Data findings**\n- 14,074 requests from 1,001 unique IPs, about 80 MB served.\n- Status codes: 200 (12,832), 404 (801), 503 (441). The only 5xx code in the data is 503.\n- All traffic has `geo.src` = US.\n- The rule is unlikely to fire on this data. The sample is historical and the rule only looks at the last 5 minutes, so it will probably stay quiet unless new 5xx traffic arrives.\n\n**Things that didn't work**\n- Some ES|QL queries on `@timestamp` and on MIN/MAX of `timestamp` returned 400 errors. I used `timestamp` and `DATE_TRUNC` instead.\n- `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty in this shell, so I did everything through the MCP Kibana tools rather than direct API calls.\n- The first version of the \"Requests over time\" panel used `@timestamp` and I corrected it to `timestamp` after checking. I haven't visually confirmed the dashboard renders the chart.",
